# 5. SINGLE-DEVICE LOGIN SECURITY — CRITICAL

This is a mandatory platform-wide requirement.

ONE ACCOUNT = ONE ACTIVE DEVICE SESSION.

A user must not be able to remain logged in on multiple devices simultaneously.

Expected behavior:

1. A student logs in on Device A.
2. The backend creates a unique active session.
3. Device A can access the platform.
4. The same student attempts to log in on Device B.
5. The backend creates a new authenticated session and atomically invalidates the previous session.
6. Device A loses authorization immediately or on its next protected request.
7. Device B becomes the only authorized active device.

Implement the same behavior for contributors and professors.

Administrators should have separately configurable security policies, with single-device enforcement as the default.

TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:

- Create a session registry in Firestore.
- Each login creates a unique server-generated session ID.
- Maintain one active session per user.
- Enforce session replacement using Firestore transactions.
- Use secure, HTTP-only, Secure and SameSite cookies where appropriate.
- Never store authentication secrets in localStorage.
- Verify Firebase ID tokens on the backend.
- Check active session status on every protected API request.
- Revoke Firebase refresh tokens when appropriate.
- Maintain a session version or revocation mechanism.
- Apply session checks to document viewer requests.
- Use short-lived document access tokens.
- Revoke previous sessions when a new login succeeds.
- Support explicit logout.
- Support administrator-forced logout.
- Record login time, session status and security events.

IMPORTANT:

Firebase authentication alone does not implement single-device login.

Implement a backend-controlled session authorization layer.

Do not rely solely on IP addresses, browser user-agent strings or browser fingerprints. These are not reliable device identity mechanisms.

A browser-based platform cannot perfectly identify physical devices or prevent all copied credentials. Make the one-active-session rule enforceable through server-side authorization.

When an old session is invalidated, display:

"Your account was signed in on another device. Please log in again."

Also provide:
- Last active session information.
- Login notifications.
- Suspicious login detection.
- Session management for administrators.

